WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.567
GloVe.

2
00:00:01.867 --> 00:00:03.700
Word2Vec predicts locally.

3
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.251
GloVe counts globally.

4
00:00:05.251 --> 00:00:07.367
Both end up with word vectors.

5
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:11.233
GloVe came from Stanford in twenty fourteen.

6
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:14.767
Word2Vec slides a window and predicts nearby words.

7
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:17.500
GloVe counts across the whole corpus first.

8
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.367
Both encode the same distributional signal.

9
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:23.433
First, GloVe builds a table.

10
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:31.133
Every row and column is a word, and each cell counts how often two words appear near each other across the entire corpus.

11
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:33.833
Flare and CME show up together often.

12
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.133
Flare and pizza almost never do.

13
00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:38.800
Here's the whole recipe.

14
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.333
Start with the corpus.

15
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:42.333
Count co-occurrences globally.

16
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:47.067
Then fit vectors so that their dot products reproduce the log of those counts.

17
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:49.767
The result is one static vector per word.

18
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:53.267
Side by side, the two differ in method.

19
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:56.233
One predicts locally, the other counts globally.

20
00:00:56.533 --> 00:00:59.133
One uses a network, the other fits a table.

21
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.653
Both are static and context-blind.

22
00:01:01.653 --> 00:01:08.833
What mattered in practice was pretrained GloVe files, the first taste of someone else's compute for your task.

23
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:12.700
Using pretrained GloVe takes a few lines.

24
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.533
One call downloads the vectors, then they're cached on your machine.

25
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:20.533
We ask for the classic analogy: king minus man plus woman.

26
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:25.467
Then we check that astronaut and cosmonaut, which are the same job, score high.

27
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:28.133
Here's a practical use.

28
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:34.512
Before paying for API embeddings in a literature search tool, baseline with pretrained GloVe.

29
00:01:34.512 --> 00:01:38.433
Average each abstract's word vectors and retrieve by cosine.

30
00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:40.600
It's crude, but free and instant.

31
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:46.667
If the paid model beats it by only two points of recall at ten, that's a finding worth escalating.

32
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:48.661
Let's recap.

33
00:01:48.661 --> 00:01:52.233
GloVe counts globally while Word2Vec predicts locally.

34
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:55.300
Its vectors are fit to log co-occurrence counts.

35
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.267
Pretrained files were the big practical win.

36
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:01.318
But they're still static and context-blind.

37
00:02:01.318 --> 00:02:02.533
Next up: attention.
